"""Business-facing context for the ten first-pass cases.

Each case follows the manager's request, the meaning of the comparison,
and the operational handoff. Numerical evidence comes from business_cases.py.
"""

EXPANSION = {
    1: dict(
        situation='A travel coordinator must book a two-night stay for a visiting team. The request has several conditions: the arrival date is fixed, the room must be suitable, the walk must be reasonable, and the booking must be refundable. Finance asks for the lowest cost that meets those requirements. The export lists prices, but its rows describe offers on particular dates rather than interchangeable hotels. The coordinator needs a shortlist the booking team can use immediately.',
        meaning='The row count is part of the answer. A duplicate can make one offer appear twice; a missing charge can make an offer appear cheaper than it is. Once the eligible rows are identified, the coordinator calculates the same two-night total for each: room nights, cleaning, and tax. Sorting the nightly rate alone would answer a different question. The shortlist should show how the original export became a set of comparable offers, so a corrected quote can be traced to the ranking.',
        action='Send the preferred offer and two backups with total cost, walking time, cancellation deadline, and the time each quote was checked. Keep excluded offers in a separate review list with the reason for exclusion. Before purchasing, verify the live price and terms. If the first option disappears, staff can apply the same eligibility and cost rule to the next one without asking the analyst to start again.'),
    2: dict(
        situation='Finance proposes a single lodging cap of 140 CU for staff travel. The cap should control spend, but it also has to work when an employee books a real trip. A company-wide average is useful for a budget forecast; it tells little about how often a traveler in a particular town will find a suitable offer under the cap. The travel manager therefore frames the question as coverage: what share of relevant quotes would the rule actually allow?',
        meaning='A mean below the cap can coexist with many exceptions because prices have a high-cost tail. Destination mix also matters. A town with many quotes can dominate the pooled average, while a town with few affordable options creates most of the approval requests. For each destination, count offers at or below 140 and divide by that town’s own quote count. Put those shares beside the distribution of prices and the number of quotes, so the manager sees both typical spend and day-to-day friction.',
        action='Keep the cap as a starting rule and define a fast exception path for destinations with limited compliant offers. Require travelers to record the best eligible offer they found, including room and cancellation requirements, rather than justify a price against the average alone. Over the next quarter, compare exceptions, final paid prices, and the destinations actually visited. That review shows whether the cap saves money without slowing routine bookings.'),
    3: dict(
        situation='A purchasing manager is considering a rule that sends routine orders to one channel: online or local shops. The business aim is to save money without making staff spend more time searching or waiting for delivery. The team collects both quotes for the same products at the same time. That pairing matters because comparing unrelated online and shop baskets would mix a channel difference with differences in what was purchased.',
        meaning='The average online-minus-offline difference tells the manager which way the paired quotes lean. The interval describes the precision of that average; the manager’s 1.50 CU tolerance describes how large a difference would justify changing the process. Those are separate questions. Individual products also vary around the average, so a single channel rule can lose money on particular items even when the overall mean favors it. Shipping, taxes, stock, and order time belong in the final purchasing cost.',
        action='Use a product-level comparison for large or urgent orders and a simple convenience rule for small purchases. Record the delivered cost and the time staff spend ordering during a short pilot. At the end, judge the policy against total purchasing effort and cost, not only the quoted price gap. The manager can then decide whether a default channel helps, which products need an exception, and whether the difference is worth enforcing.'),
    4: dict(
        situation='A workshop manager must promise completion times before new batches reach the floor. Batch size is known when a job is booked, so planners can use it to forecast labor and delivery dates. The manager’s problem is operational: a late promise can disappoint a customer, while a large safety margin can waste capacity. The team needs a rule that is accurate enough to schedule work and simple enough for planners to apply.',
        meaning='The fitted straight line gives a quick estimate of how minutes rise with batch size. The team then looks at actual times and forecast misses across the observed size range. Larger batches show a different pattern and more variation, so average fit alone is a poor basis for deadline promises. Candidate rules are compared on held-back batches using the same error measure. The smallest numerical score matters only if it improves staffing or reduces costly late jobs enough to justify extra complexity.',
        action='Begin with a forecast for batches similar to those already observed and add a buffer tied to the cost of lateness. Send unusually large or unfamiliar batches for manual review. During a shadow trial, planners record their usual estimate, the model estimate, the actual finish time, and any deadline miss. Management can then adopt the rule that makes better promises in routine work, with a clear trigger for human judgment.'),
    5: dict(
        situation='A calibration manager wants to know whether powered fixtures are worth buying for more rigs. Existing fixture rigs have higher output, but they are also older on average. The capital decision is about future purchases for a particular mix of rigs, not about explaining one headline gap. The manager needs an estimate of extra output that can be paired with equipment price, installation downtime, maintenance, and remaining service life.',
        meaning='The raw fixture gap and the age-adjusted gap answer different questions because they compare different rig mixes. Looking at rigs of similar age brings the result closer to the investment decision. The fuller analysis lets the fixture difference vary with service age, so finance can calculate payback for the ages it actually operates. A gain that pays for itself on a heavily used rig may not repay the cost on a rig nearing retirement.',
        action='Identify rig ages and operating conditions where the expected extra output could cover the purchase. Pilot fixtures on a small set of suitable rigs under a recorded assignment rule. Measure hourly output, downtime, maintenance, and technician time over the same follow-up period. The investment memo can then show expected payback by rig type and explain which assets should be considered first.'),
    6: dict(
        situation='A dispatch firm quotes jobs before the final service cost is known. Underquoting hurts margin; quoting too high may lose the customer. Dispatchers know some facts at intake, such as job size and route, but they learn others only after the work is done. The firm wants a forecast that uses information available when a quote must be issued and that improves the commercial decision, not merely a score on a historical file.',
        meaning='The team compares a simple baseline with a selected forecasting procedure on jobs kept out of model choice. Root-mean-square error puts extra weight on large misses, which is useful when big surprises matter. It still does not distinguish an underquote from an overquote in terms of profit. The pricing team should therefore read the error score alongside the pattern of misses on costly jobs, the margin rule, and how often staff would need to override a suggested quote.',
        action='Try the procedure in a limited quoting workflow. Record its proposed quote, the dispatcher’s final quote, realized cost, customer acceptance, and the reason for each override. Route jobs with missing or unusual inputs to a review path. Decide in advance what would count as success: fewer damaging underquotes, acceptable win rates, and manageable staff effort. That turns a model comparison into a controlled operating decision.'),
    7: dict(
        situation='A team-performance manager sees that users of a new tool outperform nonusers and is asked to fund a wider rollout. Managers chose the first recipients, so the observed gap includes both the tool’s contribution and the starting differences between teams. The budget question is more precise: would offering the tool to eligible teams improve performance enough to cover licenses, training, and disruption?',
        meaning='The user-versus-nonuser average is a useful signal to investigate, but it is not the gain the purchase would create. The manager needs to define the offer, the team unit, the performance measure, and the period before comparing outcomes. A pilot that randomly offers the tool to some eligible teams gives a clearer comparison with usual operations. Track actual use as a separate implementation measure, since the decision to offer a tool and the decision to use it answer different questions.',
        action='Set a minimum gain per team that would repay the rollout cost. Assign the offer among eligible teams, keep the assignment list, and measure the same outcome on the same schedule for both groups. Report the result by offer, plus adoption and training effort. If the gain clears the threshold, expand in stages; if adoption is weak, improve implementation before buying for the whole organization.'),
    8: dict(
        situation='A program office wants to expand a service that people entered under an existing selection process. Participants have better outcomes, but they also entered with different needs and characteristics. The manager must decide whether the program helps the eligible population and where expansion would be most useful. The analysis begins with the order of events: what was known before the offer, what changed after participation, and what outcome the program is meant to improve.',
        meaning='The team compares participants with nonparticipants who had similar pre-program characteristics, then combines those comparisons for the population in the expansion plan. This changes the answer from a raw participant gap to a defined program estimate. The weights matter because a network-wide rollout may have a different mix of people from today’s participants. Show the manager how much of the population has comparable people in both groups and where the comparison is thin.',
        action='Put the adjusted estimate, target population, and thin comparison groups in one budget memo. Investigate why people entered the program and which important pre-program factors were recorded. For groups where the existing data provide little comparison, design a measured pilot rather than extending the same average mechanically. That gives the office a decision for today and a concrete plan for stronger evidence tomorrow.'),
    9: dict(
        situation='A service network offered process coaching to selected facilities. The coached sites have better outcomes, and the director is considering network-wide funding. The facilities were selected partly for their starting conditions, so the director needs to know how coaching would perform across the facilities covered by the budget. A clear trial description comes first: eligibility, start date, coaching package, comparison sites, and outcome window.',
        meaning='The analysis predicts each facility’s outcome under coaching and under usual operations using characteristics measured before coaching. It averages those paired predictions over the proposed rollout population. That estimate answers a more useful question than the observed gap between two selected groups. The director also needs to know which facility types appear in both groups. Where a type is found only among coached sites, a planned pilot can provide a stronger basis for that part of the expansion.',
        action='Show estimated benefit per facility beside coaching cost, staff time, and the number of comparable sites. Name the population the recommendation covers: current coached sites or all eligible sites. Reserve a second phase for facilities where evidence is sparse, with a recorded offer rule and the same outcome measure. The result becomes a staged spending plan rather than a single network-wide yes or no.'),
    10: dict(
        situation='A service network introduced a new scheduling protocol at selected facilities. Performance rose afterward, and the operations director wants to expand it. Other facilities also changed over the same period, so the team compares both groups before and after rollout. The decision depends on the improvement beyond the movement already occurring across the network, together with the cost of training and disruption.',
        meaning='The four averages make the calculation visible: adopter performance before and after, and comparison performance before and after. Subtract the comparison group’s change from the adopters’ change to estimate the additional improvement. This approach uses the comparison facilities as a picture of the wider trend. The director should check the actual start of training, whether other sites copied the practice, and whether a separate change reached adopters at the same time.',
        action='Compare the additional improvement with the minimum gain needed to justify implementation. Review the facility timeline with operations staff, including staffing changes and the sites that remain in the report. If the comparison fits the rollout history, expand in stages under a recorded timetable and track the same outcome for new sites. This creates a practical way to update the decision as implementation proceeds.'),
}

# A final step back from the case: what the manager can reuse in other decisions.
BIGGER_PICTURE = {
    1: 'A reliable recommendation begins before any calculation. The team has to agree what one row represents, which options meet the business requirements, and what total cost means. Those choices shape every later chart or model. The booking shortlist is one example of a general management habit: make the decision rule explicit, preserve the path from raw records to the options considered, and give the person taking action a result they can use. The next lesson asks what the full spread of those options tells us.',
    2: 'Business policies affect people at the edges of a distribution, not only at its center. A cap can meet an average spending target while creating frequent exceptions for particular destinations. The useful management question is how a rule performs across the work the organization actually does. This idea travels to inventory limits, service targets, credit rules, and staffing plans: show both the overall result and who or what encounters the rule most often. The next lesson asks how large an observed difference needs to be before changing a policy.',
    3: 'A numerical difference matters only in relation to a decision. Matching products makes the comparison fair; the interval shows how precisely the average is estimated; the manager’s tolerance translates that estimate into a possible purchasing rule. The same sequence applies to vendor selection, process changes, and price tests. State the action threshold before reading the result, then judge the complete cost of acting. The next lesson extends that logic from comparing current options to forecasting outcomes for work that has not yet arrived.',
    4: 'A forecast becomes valuable when it changes an operating decision. The workshop does not need the most impressive curve; it needs dependable staffing and delivery promises. That means choosing an error measure tied to the cost of a miss, testing the rule on new batches, and defining when staff should review an unusual job. The principle applies to demand, delivery, and capacity forecasts. The next lesson asks a related question: when conditions differ across assets, which prediction or comparison should guide an investment?',
    5: 'An average return is rarely the whole capital case. The right purchase can depend on asset age, utilization, installation cost, and time left to recover the investment. Showing the gain for a specified rig turns a regression result into a portfolio decision: where should limited funds go first? This way of thinking also applies to training, maintenance, and technology upgrades. The next lesson shifts from estimating an equipment relationship to building a procedure that can support many future decisions at the moment they arise.',
    6: 'A model score is a step toward an operating policy, not the policy itself. The firm has to decide which mistakes hurt margins, which inputs exist when a quote is made, and how staff will handle unusual jobs. Deployment is therefore a workflow decision involving pricing, customer response, monitoring, and human judgment. The same pattern applies to risk scores and demand forecasts. The next lesson turns to a different question: whether offering a new tool actually changes performance, rather than merely predicting which teams perform well.',
    7: 'A business can predict which teams will do well and still be uncertain whether buying a tool will help them. The purchase decision calls for a comparison of what happens with and without an offer, tied to a budget threshold. A planned pilot makes that comparison part of the rollout rather than an afterthought. The wider lesson applies to training, software, incentives, and service changes: define the intervention and the gain needed to justify it before expansion. The next lesson tackles the same decision when an experiment was not built into the program.',
    8: 'When a program already exists, the organization may have to learn from how it was assigned in ordinary operations. The crucial work is to reconstruct the selection process, compare units with similar starting conditions, and give weight to the people the decision will affect. That connects the estimate to an expansion plan. It also shows where a new pilot would be valuable. The next lesson turns this reasoning into a facility-level evaluation and asks how to report a result for the exact population covered by a budget.',
    9: 'The target population is a management choice as much as an analytical one. A result for facilities already coached can differ from a result for every facility the network might fund. The director should see which sites have credible comparisons, what coaching costs, and where a second phase would produce useful evidence. This makes an adjusted estimate part of a staged investment plan. The next lesson considers another common rollout pattern: a change introduced at some sites while the whole network is moving over time.',
    10: 'A rising outcome after a rollout does not by itself tell a director what the rollout contributed. The comparison must account for the change already happening elsewhere, then connect the remaining gain to implementation cost and a scaling plan. The four-cell table makes that reasoning visible to operations staff. Across the course, the recurring discipline is the same: define the business action, build a comparison that speaks to it, read the result in useful units, and decide what to do next.',
}
